research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-5165-    58	
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-5166-    59	**Given:** An infinite cardinal $\kappa$, in ZF. No choice principle is assumed. For ordinals $\xi, \eta$ write $\max(\xi,\eta)$ for the $\subseteq$-larger of the two, which exists by comparability.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-5167-    60	
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-5168-    61	[L1] For a well-orderable $X$: $X \approx \lvert X \rvert$, the value is a cardinal, equinumerous sets receive the same one, $\lvert \alpha \rvert \le \alpha$, and $\lvert \alpha \rvert = \alpha$ exactly when $\alpha$ is a cardinal ([[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-5169-    62	
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log:5170:    63	[L2] $\times$ respects $\approx$, and $\alpha \times \beta$ carries an explicit well-order for ordinals $\alpha, \beta$ ([[lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-5171-    64	
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-5172-    65	[L3] For cardinals, $\kappa \le \lambda$ iff $\kappa \preceq \lambda$; and $A \preceq B$ with both well-orderable gives $\lvert A \rvert \le \lvert B \rvert$ (claim (a) of [[lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-5173-    66	
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log:5174:    67	[L4] $\omega$ is a cardinal, every natural number is a cardinal, every infinite cardinal is a limit ordinal, and for $m, n \in \omega$ the value $m \otimes n$ is again a natural number ([[thm-cardinal-arithmetic-agrees-with-finite-counting]], [[def-limit-ordinal]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-5175-    68	
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-5176-    69	[L5] $\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N} \approx \mathbb{N}$, that is $\omega \times \omega \approx \omega$ ([[thm-n-cross-n-countable]], [[def-countable]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-5177-    70	
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-5178-    71	[L6] Every well-order is order isomorphic to exactly one ordinal, its order type; an order isomorphism is a bijection and carries the initial segment below a point onto the initial segment below its image ([[thm-mostowski-collapse]], [[def-order-isomorphism]], [[def-initial-segment]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-5179-    72	
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-5180-    73	[L7] If $S \subseteq W$ for a well-order $(W,<)$ satisfies "$W_{<a} \subseteq S$ implies $a \in S$" for every $a \in W$, then $S = W$ ([[thm-transfinite-induction]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-5181-    74	
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-5182-    75	[L8] Ordinals: elements of ordinals are ordinals, $\alpha \notin \alpha$, $\alpha \subseteq \beta$ iff $\alpha \in \beta$ or $\alpha = \beta$, trichotomy holds, every nonempty set of ordinals has an $\in$-least element, and every set of ordinals is well ordered by $\in$ ([[lem-ordinal-basics]], [[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]], [[def-ordinal]], [[def-well-order]]).
