research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-6426-items/ex-an-aleph-fixed-point.md:93:**Why a fixed point is not a contradiction.** $\beta \le \aleph_\beta$ holds everywhere and the operation is strictly increasing, but neither forces $\beta < \aleph_\beta$: strict increase compares the values at two different indices and says nothing about the value at one index. The tower converges exactly because at a limit the value is the supremum of the earlier ones, and the supremum of the tower is what the tower was climbing towards.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-6427-items/ex-an-aleph-fixed-point.md:97:**Its cofinality is the smallest an infinite cardinal can have.** $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha) = \aleph_0$ says $\alpha$ is reached by an $\omega$-indexed family, which is exactly how it was built. So this fixed point is singular, and by [[cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power]] it is therefore not a possible value of $2^{\aleph_0}$. Nothing above claims it is the least fixed point.
