research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-6600-    79	1.1 Apply [L2] to the rule sending a function $h$ whose domain is an ordinal to $\aleph_{\bigcup \operatorname{ran}(h)}$, which is given by a formula; this yields exactly one class function $T$, defined at every ordinal, with $T(\beta) = \aleph_{\bigcup \operatorname{ran}(T \restriction \beta)}$, and in particular $T(0) = \aleph_{\bigcup \varnothing} = \aleph_0$. [L1, L2, L5]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-6601-    80	
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-6602-    81	2.1 By induction along [L3] on $n \in \omega$, the statement "$T(k) \in T(k+1)$ for every $k \le n$, and $T(n+1) = \aleph_{T(n)}$" holds for every $n$. At $n = 0$: $\operatorname{ran}(T \restriction 1) = \{T(0)\}$ by [L4], so $T(1) = \aleph_{T(0)} = \aleph_{\aleph_0}$, and $0 \in \aleph_0$ gives $\aleph_0 \in \aleph_{\aleph_0}$ by the strict increase in [L1], that is $T(0) \in T(1)$. At $n+1$: the statement at $n$ makes $T(0) \subseteq \cdots \subseteq T(n+1)$ by [L5], so $\bigcup \operatorname{ran}(T \restriction (n+2)) = T(n+1)$ and $T(n+2) = \aleph_{T(n+1)}$; and $T(n) \in T(n+1)$ together with strict increase gives $\aleph_{T(n)} \in \aleph_{T(n+1)}$, that is $T(n+1) \in T(n+2)$. [step 1.1, L1, L3, L4, L5]
--
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-6613-    92	
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-6614-    93	**Why a fixed point is not a contradiction.** $\beta \le \aleph_\beta$ holds everywhere and the operation is strictly increasing, but neither forces $\beta < \aleph_\beta$: strict increase compares the values at two different indices and says nothing about the value at one index. The tower converges exactly because at a limit the value is the supremum of the earlier ones, and the supremum of the tower is what the tower was climbing towards.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-6615-    94	
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-6616-    95	**Nothing is chosen, and nothing beyond Replacement is used.** The tower is a definable $\omega$-indexed family, Replacement makes its range a set, and every step of the verification is a computation. So the whole example is a theorem of ZF, like the aleph hierarchy it is built from.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-6617-    96	
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f2-cantor-hessenberg.log-6618-    97	**Its cofinality is the smallest an infinite cardinal can have.** $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha) = \aleph_0$ says $\alpha$ is reached by an $\omega$-indexed family, which is exactly how it was built. So this fixed point is singular, and by [[cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power]] it is therefore not a possible value of $2^{\aleph_0}$. Nothing above claims it is the least fixed point.
