research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-6752-1.2 By [L3] and [L4] the ordinals $1$ and $\aleph_0 = \omega$ are cardinals, $\aleph_0$ is infinite, $1 \in \aleph_0$ and hence $1 \le \aleph_0$, and $1 \ne \aleph_0$ by [L5]. [L3, L4, L5]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-6753-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-6754-2.1 By [L1] with $\nu = \aleph_0$ and $\rho = \aleph_0$, $\aleph_0 \oplus \aleph_0 = \aleph_0$; and by [L2] and [L1] with $\rho = 1$, $1 \oplus \aleph_0 = \aleph_0 \oplus 1 = \aleph_0$. [step 1.2, L1, L2]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-6755-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-6756-3.1 So the hypothesis of the assumed claim holds at $\kappa = \aleph_0$, $\lambda = 1$, $\mu = \aleph_0$, and the claim would give $\aleph_0 = 1$, which step 1.2 forbids; therefore cardinal addition is not cancellative. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1, discharge-contradiction] ∎
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-6757-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-6758-## Remarks
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-6759-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:6760:**The finite case really is cancellative, and nothing above contradicts it.** For $m, n, k \in \omega$ read as cardinals, $m \oplus k = n \oplus k$ is $m +_{\mathbb{N}} k = n +_{\mathbb{N}} k$ by [[thm-cardinal-arithmetic-agrees-with-finite-counting]], and Peano addition is cancellative. The witness above is forced to use an infinite $\mu$, and once $\mu$ is infinite every $\kappa \le \mu$ gives the same sum.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-6761-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:6762:**Multiplication fails in the same way, and for the same reason.** [[cor-cardinal-absorption]] also gives $\aleph_0 \otimes \aleph_0 = \aleph_0 = 1 \otimes \aleph_0$ with $1 \ne \aleph_0$, so $\otimes$ is not cancellative either, even away from the trivial obstruction at $\mu = 0$.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-6763-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:6764:**What survives.** Monotonicity survives: $\kappa \le \lambda$ still gives $\kappa \oplus \mu \le \lambda \oplus \mu$ ([[lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws]]). It is the *strict* form that fails, and cancellation is exactly the strict form in disguise. Exponentiation is the one place on this page where a strict increase survives at every cardinal, and that is [[thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor]].
