research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-6839-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-6840-**Why the collapse happens.** For an infinite exponent $\mu$: once the base is at least $2$ and at most $2^{\mu}$, raising it to the power $\mu$ gives the same value, because $(2^{\mu})^{\mu} = 2^{\mu \otimes \mu} = 2^{\mu}$ squeezes the chain shut. So for infinite $\mu$ strict monotonicity in the base can only survive where the base is allowed to exceed $2^{\mu}$, and it fails on the whole interval below it; for finite exponents $\ge 1$ the collapse does not occur, which is why the witness above takes $\mu = \aleph_0$. That is a fact about [[thm-hessenberg]] as much as about exponentiation.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-6841-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-6842-**The weak form is not damaged.** $\kappa \le \lambda \Rightarrow \kappa^{\mu} \le \lambda^{\mu}$ remains true, and so does Cantor's strict inequality $\kappa < 2^{\kappa}$. What fails is the strict form in the base, and it fails at the smallest infinite instance.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-6843-
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-6844-**A second casualty of the same computation.** The chain in step 2.1 also shows $\aleph_0^{\aleph_0} = 2^{\aleph_0}$, so raising $\aleph_0$ to its own power adds nothing beyond taking the power set of $\omega$. The companion page carries that computation on its own, together with the corresponding value for $\lvert \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{R}} \rvert$.
