research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7081:items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:4:title: "Every infinite cardinal is $\\aleph_\\alpha$ for exactly one ordinal $\\alpha$, in ZF; and, assuming the Axiom of Choice, every infinite set is equinumerous with exactly one aleph"
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7082:items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:48:**(b) Assuming the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Every infinite
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7083:items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:58:**Given:** ZF; the Axiom of Choice only in clause (b).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7084:items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:60:[L1] The operation $\alpha \mapsto \aleph_\alpha$ is defined at every ordinal, takes infinite cardinal values, is strictly increasing, satisfies $\aleph_0 = \omega$, $\aleph_{\alpha+1} = \aleph_\alpha^{+}$, $\aleph_\lambda = \bigcup\{\aleph_\beta : \beta \in \lambda\}$ at limits, and satisfies $\alpha \le \aleph_\alpha$ ([[cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7085:items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:62:[L2] $\kappa^{+}$ is the least cardinal strictly above the cardinal $\kappa$ ([[lem-successor-cardinal-exists]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7086:items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:72:[L7] Assuming the Axiom of Choice, every set carries a well-order ([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7087:items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:86:3.1 In each of the three cases of [L4] this forces $\kappa = \aleph_\alpha$: if $\alpha = 0$ then $\omega = \aleph_0 \le \kappa \le \aleph_0$ by [L5], so $\kappa = \aleph_0$; if $\alpha = \beta + 1$ then $\aleph_\beta < \kappa$ by step 2.1 and $\kappa$ is a cardinal, so $\aleph_{\beta+1} = \aleph_\beta^{+} \le \kappa$ by [L2], while $\kappa \le \aleph_\alpha$ gives the reverse; and if $\alpha$ is a limit then $\aleph_\beta \subseteq \kappa$ for every $\beta \in \alpha$ by step 2.1, so $\aleph_\alpha = \bigcup\{\aleph_\beta : \beta \in \alpha\} \subseteq \kappa$ by [L1], again with the reverse inequality already in hand. [step 2.1, L1, L2, L3, L4, L5]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7088:items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:88:4.1 Claim (a) is step 3.1 with the uniqueness of step 1.2; and claim (b) follows: assuming the Axiom of Choice an infinite set $X$ is well-orderable by [L7], so $\lvert X \rvert$ exists by [L6] and is not a natural number, since $X \approx \lvert X \rvert$ would then make $X$ finite by [L8], whence $\lvert X \rvert$ is an infinite cardinal by [L5] and $X \approx \lvert X \rvert = \aleph_\alpha$ for exactly one $\alpha$, uniqueness holding because $X \approx \aleph_\alpha$ forces $\aleph_\alpha = \lvert X \rvert$ by [L6]. [step 1.2, step 3.1, L5, L6, L7, L8] ∎
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7089:items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:94:**Clause (b) is exactly as strong as the well-ordering theorem.** If every infinite set were equinumerous with an aleph then every set would be well-orderable, since an aleph is an ordinal, and that is equivalent to the Axiom of Choice ([[cor-ac-iff-well-ordering]]). So clause (b) is not a theorem of ZF, and it is stated with its hypothesis rather than proved.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7090:items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:96:**What is enumerated and what is not.** The alephs enumerate the infinite cardinals in increasing order. They do **not** enumerate the values of the power operation: $2^{\aleph_0}$ is an aleph by clause (a), but which one is not settled by the axioms in use here, and nothing on this page or its companion asserts a value.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7091-items/thm-konig.md:4:title: "König's theorem: assuming the Axiom of Choice, if $\\kappa_i < \\lambda_i$ for every $i \\in I$ then $\\sum_{i \\in I} \\kappa_i < \\prod_{i \\in I} \\lambda_i$"
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7092-items/thm-konig.md:42:**Assume the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $I$ be a set and
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7093-items/thm-konig.md:61:**Given:** The Axiom of Choice; a set $I$; families of cardinals $(\kappa_i)_{i \in I}$, $(\lambda_i)_{i \in I}$ with $\kappa_i < \lambda_i$ for every $i$. Write $S = \bigcup_{i \in I}(\{i\} \times \kappa_i)$ and $P$ for the set of functions $f$ on $I$ with $f(i) \in \lambda_i$ for every $i$.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7094-items/thm-konig.md:63:[L1] $\sum_{i \in I}\kappa_i = \lvert S\rvert$ and $\prod_{i \in I}\lambda_i = \lvert P\rvert$, both defined because the Axiom of Choice well-orders every set ([[def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product]], [[thm-well-ordering-theorem]], [[def-cardinal-arithmetic]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7095-items/thm-konig.md:95:**The set form of the theorem implies the Axiom of Choice outright, in one line.** Suppose it were true that for families of **sets** with $A_i \prec B_i$ for every $i$ one had $\bigsqcup_i A_i \prec \prod_i B_i$. Given nonempty sets $B_i$, take $A_i = \varnothing$: then $A_i \preceq B_i$ and $A_i \not\approx B_i$, so $A_i \prec B_i$; the conclusion gives $\varnothing \prec \prod_i B_i$, hence $\prod_i B_i \not\approx \varnothing$ and $\prod_i B_i \ne \varnothing$, which is exactly the product formulation of [[def-axiom-of-choice]]. So the hypothesis of this theorem is not an artefact of the proof, and the version stated above, for **cardinals**, is the one that can be written down at all without presupposing choice somewhere.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7096-items/thm-konig.md:97:**Where the diagonal is.** Step 5.1 says that the $i$-th block of $S$, which has only $\kappa_i$ members, cannot exhaust the $\lambda_i$ possible values in the $i$-th coordinate. Step 6.1 assembles the omitted values into a single element of the product. This is Cantor's diagonal argument with an arbitrary index set in place of $\mathbb{N}$, and with the two-element set replaced by $\lambda_i$; the one thing it needs beyond Cantor's version is the simultaneous selection, which is where the Axiom of Choice is spent the second time.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7097-items/thm-konig.md:99:**What it is used for on this page.** With $\lambda_i$ constant the product becomes an exponential, and the resulting inequality bounds the cofinality of a power from below; that consequence is [[cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power]], and it is the only ZFC constraint on $2^{\aleph_0}$ established here.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7098-items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:57:nothing about how *large* the next cardinal is compared with $2^{\kappa}$; that
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7099-items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:96:**The notation collides with the ordinal successor, and this page keeps them apart.** $\alpha^{+} = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$ is the **ordinal** successor ([[def-ordinal]]) and is almost never a cardinal: an infinite cardinal is a limit ordinal, so $\kappa \cup \{\kappa\}$ is not one. The cardinal successor is $\aleph(\kappa)$, which is much larger. Where an item on this page writes the superscript on an ordinal letter, as [[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]] does with $\alpha^{+}$, it carries the published ordinal meaning $\alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$; the cardinal successor is always written $\aleph(\kappa)$ on this page.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7100-items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:4:title: "FALSE: $2^{\\aleph_0} = \\aleph_\\omega$"
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7101-items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:14:short: "$\\operatorname{cf}(2^{\\aleph_0}) > \\aleph_0 = \\operatorname{cf}(\\aleph_\\omega)$"
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7102-items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:42:**FALSE.** Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). The continuum
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7103-items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:45:$$2^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_\omega$$
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7104-items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:49:The claim is plausible because ZFC really does leave the value of $2^{\aleph_0}$
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7105-items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:57:**Given:** The Axiom of Choice.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7106-items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:59:[L1] $\operatorname{cf}(2^{\kappa}) > \kappa$ for every infinite cardinal $\kappa$; in particular $\operatorname{cf}(2^{\aleph_0}) > \aleph_0$ ([[cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power]], [[def-cofinality]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7107:items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:63:[L3] $2^{\aleph_0}$ is a cardinal strictly above $\aleph_0$ ([[thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor]], [[def-cardinal]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7108-items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:71:1.1 Suppose, for contradiction, that $2^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_\omega$. [assume-contra]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7109-items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:73:1.2 By [L1] and [L3], $\operatorname{cf}(2^{\aleph_0}) > \aleph_0$. [L1, L3]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7110-items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:77:2.1 Equal ordinals have equal cofinalities, so the assumption turns step 1.3 into $\operatorname{cf}(2^{\aleph_0}) = \aleph_0$, contradicting step 1.2 by [L4]; therefore $2^{\aleph_0} \ne \aleph_\omega$. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 1.3, L4, discharge-contradiction] ∎
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7111-items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:83:**Which values remain possible is a different question, and is not settled here.** The cofinality constraint excludes candidate values whose cofinality is $\aleph_0$; it does not identify the value of $2^{\aleph_0}$, and the choice ledger at the end of the main page records what is and is not decided.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7112-items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:85:**The parallel false claim about $\aleph_1$ is of a different kind.** "$2^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_1$" is not refutable here at all: it is the continuum hypothesis, and neither it nor its negation follows from anything on this page or on the pages this one rests on, as [[rem-cardinal-arithmetic-choice-ledger]] records. That difference is exactly why the present statement is on the page and the other is not: a false statement in this library carries a refutation, and a refutation must be a proof.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7113-items/thm-cardinal-comparability-iff-ac.md:4:title: "Comparability of arbitrary sets, that any two sets admit an injection one way or the other, is equivalent to the Axiom of Choice"
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7114-items/thm-cardinal-comparability-iff-ac.md:44:**(1)** The Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7115-items/thm-cardinal-comparability-iff-ac.md:61:[L2] Over ZF the Axiom of Choice is equivalent to the well-ordering theorem, that every set can be well ordered ([[cor-ac-iff-well-ordering]], [[def-well-order]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7116-items/thm-cardinal-comparability-iff-ac.md:63:[L3] Assuming the Axiom of Choice, every set carries a well-order ([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7117-items/thm-cardinal-comparability-iff-ac.md:75:1.1 Assume (1). Given sets $A$ and $B$, both are well-orderable by [L3], so $\lvert A\rvert$ and $\lvert B\rvert$ exist with $A \approx \lvert A\rvert$ and $B \approx \lvert B\rvert$ by [L4]; trichotomy in [L5] gives $\lvert A\rvert \subseteq \lvert B\rvert$ or $\lvert B\rvert \subseteq \lvert A\rvert$, and composing the corresponding inclusion with the two bijections gives an injection one way or the other by [L6], which is (2). [L3, L4, L5, L6]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7118-items/thm-cardinal-comparability-iff-ac.md:77:1.2 Assume (2), and let $A$ be any set. Put $\kappa = \aleph(A)$, which by [L1] admits no injection into $A$; comparability applied to $A$ and $\kappa$ therefore leaves an injection $j : A \to \kappa$. Defining $a <_A b :\iff j(a) \in j(b)$ transports the well-order of the ordinal $\kappa$ back to $A$: irreflexivity, transitivity and trichotomy are immediate from injectivity, and a nonempty $S \subseteq A$ has the $<_A$-least element $j^{-1}$ of the $\in$-least element of $j[S]$, which exists by [L5]. So every set can be well ordered, and (1) follows by [L2]. [L1, L2, L5, L6]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7119-items/thm-cardinal-comparability-iff-ac.md:85:**What is not being claimed.** The theorem does not say that two sets are always comparable, nor that they are not. It says that "always comparable" and "the Axiom of Choice" are the same assumption over ZF. Whether ZF alone refutes comparability is a different question, not addressed here.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7120:items/thm-cardinal-comparability-iff-ac.md:87:**Where this sits relative to the rest of the page.** Trichotomy for the alephs is free: they are ordinals, and [[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]] is a theorem of ZF. The step that costs the Axiom of Choice is getting from an arbitrary set to an aleph in the first place, which is clause (b) of [[thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph]]. This theorem is that observation sharpened into an equivalence.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7121-items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:4:title: "The sum $\\sum_{i \\in I} \\kappa_i$ and the product $\\prod_{i \\in I} \\kappa_i$ of an indexed family of cardinals, defined under the Axiom of Choice"
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7122-items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:41:**Assume the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $I$ be a set and
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7123-items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:45:$$\textstyle\bigsqcup_{i \in I} \kappa_i \;:=\; \bigcup_{i \in I} \big(\{i\} \times \kappa_i\big), \qquad \prod^{\mathrm{set}}_{i \in I} \kappa_i \;:=\; \{\, f : f \text{ is a function on } I \text{ with } f(i) \in \kappa_i \text{ for all } i \in I \,\},$$
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7124-items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:50:$$\sum_{i \in I} \kappa_i \;:=\; \Big\lvert \textstyle\bigsqcup_{i \in I} \kappa_i \Big\rvert, \qquad \prod_{i \in I} \kappa_i \;:=\; \Big\lvert \prod^{\mathrm{set}}_{i \in I} \kappa_i \Big\rvert .$$
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7125-items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:53:cardinalities of sets that ZF does not well-order. Under the Axiom of Choice
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7126-items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:64:$\prod^{\mathrm{set}}_{i \in 2} \kappa_i$ onto $\kappa_0 \times \kappa_1$, with
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7127-items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:72:$\prod^{\mathrm{set}}_{i \in I} \kappa = {}^{I}\kappa$, so
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7128-items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:74:$$\sum_{i \in I} \kappa = \lambda \otimes \kappa, \qquad \prod_{i \in I} \kappa = \kappa^{\lambda},$$
