research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7133-items/cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power.md:34:**Assume the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $\kappa$ be an
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7134-items/cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power.md:37:**(a)** $\kappa < \kappa^{\operatorname{cf}(\kappa)}$
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7135-items/cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power.md:40:**(b)** $\operatorname{cf}(2^{\kappa}) > \kappa$;
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7136-items/cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power.md:42:**(c)** in particular $\operatorname{cf}(2^{\aleph_0}) > \aleph_0$
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7137-items/cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power.md:46:ZFC rather than quoted.** It rules out every value of $2^{\aleph_0}$ whose
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7138-items/cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power.md:51:**Given:** The Axiom of Choice and an infinite cardinal $\kappa$.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7139-items/cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power.md:55:[L2] For a constant family, $\prod_{i \in I}\kappa = \kappa^{\lvert I\rvert}$; and $\sum_{i \in I}\kappa_i = \lvert \bigcup_{i \in I}(\{i\} \times \kappa_i)\rvert$ ([[def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7140-items/cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power.md:59:[L4] For cardinals $\kappa \le \lambda$ iff $\kappa \preceq \lambda$; $A \preceq B$ with both well-orderable gives $\lvert A\rvert \le \lvert B\rvert$; $\mu^{\nu} \le \mu^{\rho}$ for $\nu \le \rho$ and $\mu \ne 0$; and $(\mu^{\nu})^{\rho} = \mu^{\nu \otimes \rho}$ ([[lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7141:items/cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power.md:63:[L6] $\kappa < 2^{\kappa}$, and $2^{\kappa}$ is a cardinal ([[thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7142-items/cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power.md:69:[L9] Assuming the Axiom of Choice every set is well-orderable, and a product of nonempty sets is nonempty ([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[def-choice-function]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7143-items/cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power.md:81:3.1 Claim (a): applying [L1] to the families $(\kappa_\xi)_{\xi \in \beta}$ and the constant family $\lambda_\xi = \kappa$, which satisfy $\kappa_\xi < \kappa$ by step 1.1, gives $\sum_{\xi \in \beta}\kappa_\xi < \prod_{\xi \in \beta}\kappa = \kappa^{\lvert \beta\rvert}$ by [L2], and $\lvert \beta\rvert = \beta = \operatorname{cf}(\kappa)$ by [L8], since $\beta$ is a cardinal; with step 2.1 this is $\kappa < \kappa^{\operatorname{cf}(\kappa)}$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, L1, L2, L8]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7144-items/cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power.md:83:4.1 Claim (b): put $\mu = 2^{\kappa}$, an infinite cardinal by [L6] and [L7] since $\omega \le \kappa < \mu$; were $\operatorname{cf}(\mu) \le \kappa$, then step 3.1 applied to $\mu$ together with [L4] and [L5] would give $\mu < \mu^{\operatorname{cf}(\mu)} \le \mu^{\kappa} = (2^{\kappa})^{\kappa} = 2^{\kappa \otimes \kappa} = 2^{\kappa} = \mu$, which [L10] forbids; so trichotomy leaves $\kappa < \operatorname{cf}(2^{\kappa})$. [step 3.1, L4, L5, L6, L7, L10]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7145-items/cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power.md:89:**What clause (b) rules out, concretely.** If $2^{\aleph_0}$ were $\aleph_\omega$ then its cofinality would be $\aleph_0$ by [[thm-regularity-of-the-alephs]], contradicting clause (c); that refutation is carried out in [[fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega]]. The same test applies to any proposed value whose cofinality can be computed. Clause (c) is a restriction and not a determination: it excludes values and selects none.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7146-items/cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power.md:91:**Why the cofinality, and not the size, is the obstruction.** Clause (a) says a cardinal is strictly smaller than itself raised to its own cofinality. Read contrapositively, a cardinal $\mu$ that **is** a power $2^{\kappa}$ cannot have its cofinality drop to or below $\kappa$, because raising $\mu$ to that exponent would not increase it. The whole argument is the interaction of two facts, König's inequality and the second exponent law, and the second is where [[thm-hessenberg]] enters.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log-7147-items/cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power.md:93:**Where the Axiom of Choice is spent here.** Three times: in the definitions of $\sum$, $\prod$ and $2^{\kappa}$; in step 2.1, to select a bijection $g(\xi) \to \lvert g(\xi)\rvert$ for every $\xi$ at once; and inside [[thm-konig]] itself. None of the three is removable by a canonical construction, which is why the whole corollary carries the hypothesis in its statement.
