research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7201:items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:61:**Given:** The Axiom of Choice, a cardinal $\kappa$, and a set $A$.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7202:items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:63:[L1] $\kappa^{\lambda} = \lvert {}^{\lambda}\kappa \rvert$, where ${}^{\lambda}\kappa$ is the set of functions $\lambda \to \kappa$ ([[def-cardinal-arithmetic]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7203:items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:73:[L6] Assuming the Axiom of Choice every set is well-orderable, hence has a cardinality ([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7204:items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:81:1.1 The map $\chi : \mathcal{P}(\kappa) \to {}^{\kappa}2$ sending $S$ to its characteristic function, $\chi_S(\xi) = 1$ for $\xi \in S$ and $\chi_S(\xi) = 0$ otherwise, has the two-sided inverse $h \mapsto h^{-1}[\{1\}]$, so it is a bijection and $\mathcal{P}(\kappa) \approx {}^{\kappa}2$. [L7]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7205:items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:85:2.1 Claim (a): by [L6] both $\mathcal{P}(\kappa)$ and ${}^{\kappa}2$ have cardinalities, equal by step 1.1 and [L3], so $\lvert \mathcal{P}(\kappa)\rvert = \lvert {}^{\kappa}2\rvert = 2^{\kappa}$ by [L1]; and for an arbitrary set $A$, $A \approx \lvert A \rvert$ by [L3] gives $\mathcal{P}(A) \approx \mathcal{P}(\lvert A \rvert)$ by [L4], hence $\lvert \mathcal{P}(A)\rvert = 2^{\lvert A \rvert}$. [step 1.1, L1, L3, L4, L6]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7206:items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:89:3.1 Therefore $\kappa < \lvert \mathcal{P}(\kappa)\rvert = 2^{\kappa}$ by trichotomy, which with step 2.1 is claim (b). [step 2.1, step 2.2, L7] ∎
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7207:items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:93:**Why $2$ and not some other base.** The characteristic function of a subset takes two values, so the power set is the function space with base $2$; that is the whole content of step 1.1, and it is why $2^{\kappa}$ rather than $\mathcal{P}$ is the object cardinal arithmetic manipulates. For infinite $\kappa$, any base $\mu$ with $2 \le \mu$ gives the same value once $\mu \le 2^{\kappa}$, by monotonicity, the second exponent law and [[thm-hessenberg]], and the companion page computes one such case; for finite $\kappa$ the bases genuinely differ, $3^{2} = 9 \ne 4 = 2^{2}$.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7208:items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:95:**No fixed point.** Clause (b) holds for **every** cardinal, so no cardinal satisfies $2^{\kappa} = \kappa$ and the hierarchy of cardinals never terminates. The corresponding statement one level up — that $\alpha \mapsto \aleph_\alpha$ has no fixed point — is **false**, and the companion page exhibits one; the two operations behave quite differently, and it is the power operation, not the successor operation, that is unboundedly expansive.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7209:items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:97:**Where the Axiom of Choice is and is not spent.** [[thm-cantor-powerset]] is choice free, and so is step 1.1. The hypothesis is used exactly twice, both times to know that a set has a cardinality: at $\mathcal{P}(\kappa)$ and at ${}^{\kappa}2$. In ZF alone, $\mathcal{P}(\omega)$ may fail to be well-orderable, and then $2^{\aleph_0}$ is not an ordinal and the inequality of clause (b) has no cardinal to compare $\kappa$ with — while the underlying statement "there is no surjection $\omega \to \mathcal{P}(\omega)$" remains a theorem.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f1-cardinal-core.log:7210:items/thm-hessenberg.md:105:**What the theorem does not say.** It is about **cardinals**, that is about well-orderable sets. "Every infinite set $A$ satisfies $A \times A \approx A$" is a strictly stronger statement, and [[thm-tarski-square]] shows it is equivalent to the Axiom of Choice. So Hessenberg's theorem is not a weaker version of Tarski's with a cheaper proof; it is the exact fragment that ZF proves.
